T-009 (epic-189-a1-project-context, REQ-007) — TDD Red evidence
Staged-tree `generate-guard-invariants.py --check` against the
INTENTIONALLY-INCOMPLETE draft candidate.

Red defect (single variable): the staged
generate-guard-invariants.py's EPIC_A1_TARGETS tuple omits the 4 RESERVED
PROTECTED-MANIFEST.md entries (resolve-project-context.{py,sh,ps1} and
generated/project-context.resolved.json), while the staged
guard-invariants.json still carries all 28. Everything else in the staged
tree is byte-identical to the Green candidate.

This is precisely the failure mode design.md's Protected-File Statement
warns about ("Editing guard-invariants.json's array without a matching
edit to generate-guard-invariants.py's own constants makes --check fail")
and the one AC-038 exists to prevent (silently dropping the resolver /
generated-projection reservations).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ cd /Users/jrmag/Projects/active/sdd-forge-wt-epic-189
$ python3 specs/epic-189-a1-project-context/human-copy/plugins/sdd-quality-loop/scripts/generate-guard-invariants.py --check
generate-guard-invariants: protected_gate_suffixes must be the exact baseline/inventory union
check_exit=1
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

How the staged tree (not the live one) is targeted
--------------------------------------------------
generate-guard-invariants.py has no --repo-root/--root flag. It anchors on
its own location:

    script_dir = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
    canonical  = script_dir.parent / "references" / "guard-invariants.json"
    outputs    = expected_outputs(data, digest, script_dir / "generated")

Invoking the STAGED COPY of the generator therefore resolves the canonical
JSON and all four generated outputs INSIDE the staging directory, and never
touches the live tree. This is the repository's own established idiom for
checking a staged inventory: tests/phase2-guard-invariants.tests.sh:24,41
does exactly this for the frozen epic-136 stage
(`run_generator "$source_loop" --check`).

tests/guard-invariants-epic-a1.tests.{sh,ps1} assert this anchoring
explicitly ("the staged generator anchors on the STAGED canonical JSON,
never the live one") so the proof cannot silently regress into a live-tree
check.

Suite results in this Red state
-------------------------------
tests/guard-invariants-epic-a1.tests.sh   PASS 70 / FAIL 5 (exit 1) — red-sh.log
tests/guard-invariants-epic-a1.tests.ps1  PASS 74 / FAIL 5 (exit 1) — red-ps1.log

The 5 failures are identical in both runtimes:
  1. TEST-021: staged generator EPIC_A1_TARGETS equals the manifest exactly
  2. TEST-021: staged-tree generate-guard-invariants.py --check passes
  3. TEST-021-MUT: pristine copy passes --check before mutation (dropped JSON entry)
  4. TEST-021-MUT: pristine copy passes --check before mutation (dropped EPIC_A1_TARGETS entry)
  5. TEST-021-MUT: pristine copy passes --check before mutation (stale generated output)

Failures 3-5 are the mutation-baseline assertions doing their job: they
refuse to report detection power while the tree under test is already
broken. An earlier draft of this suite omitted those baselines, and in the
Red state its three mutation assertions all "passed" vacuously — the
mutated copy failed --check only because the unmutated copy already did.
That is the two-guards-mask-each-other / zero-detection-power defect, and
it was fixed before this evidence was recorded.

The ps1 tally is 4 higher than the sh tally by design: the PowerShell twin
reads EPIC_A1_TARGETS / PHASE2_TARGETS / BASELINE_SUFFIXES / REQUIRED_TOP_LEVEL
by PARSING the module source, so it adds four "is parseable" guard
assertions that the .sh twin (which IMPORTS the module and reads live
objects) has no analogue for. That divergence is the deliberate variant
axis documented in the ps1 suite header.
